WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.160
Speaker 1: Welcome back to the show.

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:03.819
Let's go back and hear from a few more people
via discord.

00:00:03.819 --> 00:00:08.639
You can join the discord at David Pakman dot
com slash discord.

00:00:08.639 --> 00:00:13.089
Let's go to Pablo from Albuquerque, New Mexico.

00:00:13.089 --> 00:00:16.640
Welcome, Pablo.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:17.850
Pablo from Albuquerque.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:18.850
Welcome.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:19.850
Please unmute.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:20.850
Configure your microphone, Your Honor.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:21.850
Speaker 3: There we go.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:22.850
There you go.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:23.850
Speaker 1: You can hear me now.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:24.850
I can.

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:25.850
Speaker 3: All right, great.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:33.840
So the last time that I called in, you mentioned
that you hadn't been to New Mexico before.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.070
And it's unfortunate because right now we
actually have some really exciting stuff.

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:43.190
You know, I don't know if you heard if we're
making higher education free for people that

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:52.940
enrolled in six and six credit courses and
then, you know, we just legalized marijuana,

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.090
cannabis.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:01:01.180
And my question is sort of tied in with that.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.180
Health care.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:06.860
Obviously, that's one thing that we've tried
and haven't been able to make much progress

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.100
with.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:16.360
Do you think that a better way of trying to
get a universal health care plan would be

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:23.200
also to go the exact same way along with like
cannabis legalization, meaning?

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.880
Mm hmm.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:31.820
Just having a Democrat or a state saying,
if you're from here, you'll get free health

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:34.200
care, you'll get universal health care.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:41.049
And then if you are from one of these other
states that also.

00:01:41.049 --> 00:01:43.520
Supports universal health care.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.380
You will have insurance covered through these
states.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:52.770
Speaker 1: So the details are a bit murky
the way you lay them out.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.840
But here's how I could see something like
this.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.940
Working states start to set up their own single
payer plans.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.620
Vermont tried to do it and it failed.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:04.270
But it was a good idea.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:05.770
Some state does it.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:06.770
Okay, great.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:09.259
Then a second state does it.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:16.580
Those states could have an agreement for sort
of a consortium of sorts where now there is

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.680
a two state single payer system.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:24.520
The only part of this that I've not yet figured
out is what happens if tons of people who

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:29.410
work in other states move to those states
just for the health care?

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:32.010
You've got to make sure that you're capturing
their taxes.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:38.830
And there are ways where I mean, listen, if
you have if you work from home for a big corporation

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:45.489
and you move from Texas to Arizona for Arizona,
single payer, in theory, you now live in Arizona.

00:02:45.489 --> 00:02:46.739
You should pay taxes in Arizona.

00:02:46.739 --> 00:02:52.500
But for a lot of smaller businesses and entrepreneurs,
you can live in one place but kind of maintain

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.600
residency in another and not pay taxes where
you're living.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.800
So that would have to be figured out.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:02.370
But I love the idea of starting single payer
or something like it at the state level and

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:07.050
as states do it, they can work as a consortium
to expand the risk pool.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:08.900
I think it's a beautiful idea.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:17.080
Speaker 3: Again, the reason that I brought
up the university, a higher university, it's

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:23.700
the way that they're also they're saying if
you've resided in New Mexico for at least

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:31.420
12 months, then you're allowed to get there
and, you know, free or subsidized health care.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:32.420
Right.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:39.830
And so but, yeah, as you say, for smaller
places, it'd be a lot harder to do, I suppose.

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:43.040
Yeah, but I think that's about it.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.790
Oh, there was one other thing I can't remember.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:47.769
Speaker 1: All right, well, let's save it
for next time.

00:03:47.769 --> 00:03:50.140
Pablo from Albuquerque, thank you for the
call.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:51.950
Very important issue

